G An outline of the data structures and algorithms that you used. Given that 
this is a data structures class, you need to always discuss any data structures
that you used and justify why you used them. For this assignment it is 
imperative that you explain your data structures and algorithms in detail, as 
it will help us understand your code since there is no single right way of 
completing this assignment.

Data Structures:
HashTable: This is the core data structure we implemented to index and search
for specific words across files and directories. We chose a hash table because
it allows us constant/close to constant time access for operations like
insert and lookup which was necessary for this project. We implemented open 
addressing with linear probing for collision resolution. Each entry in the hash 
table stores a string key (the word) and a vector of WordOccurrence structs. 
The WordOccurrence struct contains three indices: fileIndex 
(index into allFilePaths), lineNumber, and lineContent 
(index into allLineTexts). Hash
tables also allow us to dynamically resize as needed with a time complexity of
0(1).

Vector of Vectors: We utilized this data structure to implement our gerp class.
We chose to use a vector of vectors because during our design checkoff, we
wanted to have constant time access, or as close as possible to make our 
program run as fast as possible. 
Each hash table entry contains a vector of WordOccurrence structs to handle 
words that appear multiple times across different files and lines. A vector 
was chosen because we need to preserve all occurrences and the order of 
insertion.

Set:
Used in printResults() to track which (file, line) combinations have already 
been printed. This ensures that if a word appears multiple times on the same 
line, we only print that line once in the results.
We chose std::set because it provides O(log n) insertion and lookup, and 
automatically handles if a given set of data is different or the same. 

Algorithms: 

Directory Traversal (Recursive DFS):
    We use a depth-first search algorithm to recursively traverse the directory 
    tree. The traverseAndIndex() function processes all files in the current 
    directory first, then recursively processes each subdirectory.
    Time Complexity: O(n) where n is the total number of files and directories.

Word Indexing:
    For each line in each file, we parse words using string streams, strip 
    leading/trailing non-alphanumeric characters using stripNonAlphaNum(), and 
    insert each cleaned word into the hash table.

stripNonAlphaNum Algorithm: Two-pointer technique that finds the first and last 
alphanumeric characters, then extracts the substring between them. This 
preserves internal punctuation (like in "can't") while removing surrounding 
punctuation.

Case-Sensitive Search:

    Uses the hash function to find the starting position, then performs linear 
    probing to find the exact key match.

Case-Insensitive Search:
    Converts the query to lowercase, then iterates through the entire hash 
    table, comparing each key after converting it to lowercase. All matching 
    entries are collected into a single result vector.

Hash Function
    We use the standard library's std::hash<std::string> function, then apply 
    modulo to fit the index within the table bounds. This provides good 
    distribution for most inputs while being fast to compute.

H Details and an explanation of how you tested the various parts of your 
classes and the program as a whole. You may reference the testing files that 
you submitted to aid in your explanation.

We used unit test to test specific classes and functions like the ones in 
processing.cpp and the HashTable class. We also diff tested on tinyData, 
small+medium+large gutenberg, which is also how we caught a few bugs our
program had experienced. We made sure the the queries were varied and tested
edge cases like 2 of the same query and queries with weird characters in 
different places. In addition, we also created our own mini-directory to test
the basic functionality of our program early on. We made sure that every file
and folder in that directory were being traversed.
 
